\itemize{
\item Without it, the document's front matter is used (see "Front matter" in
\code{\link[=pub_doc]{pub_doc()}}), which the viewer shows too.
\item It returns what a document can hold: sections, outputs or lists of them (see
\code{\link[=pub_doc]{pub_doc()}}).
\item \code{doc} is the document with the render-time metadata (see \code{\link[=pub_render]{pub_render()}}) and
\code{format} the output format.
\item Its sections are unnumbered (the numbers of the body start at 1), while its
tables and figures count in the caption numbers.
\item End it with \code{\link[=pub_pagebreak]{pub_pagebreak()}} to start the table of contents on a new page.
}
\item \code{toc}: \code{TRUE} adds a table of contents of the headings up to level \code{toc_depth}
after the front matter (or title block).
\itemize{
\item It is left out if the document has no sections, or if the document or the front
matter places a \code{\link[=pub_toc]{pub_toc()}} itself.
\item Word fills it in when its fields are updated.
\item For lists of figures and tables, place \code{pub_toc(what = "figures")} or
\code{pub_toc(what = "tables")}, e.g. in the front matter.
}
\item \code{number_sections}: \code{FALSE} leaves every heading unnumbered (in Word the "Heading n
Unnumbered" styles).
\item \code{labels}: the words before the caption numbers, \code{list(table, figure)}.
\item \code{page}: a \code{\link[=pub_page]{pub_page()}}.
\itemize{
\item By default, DOCX keeps the page of the reference document and PDF is A4 portrait
with margins of 2.5 cm (2 cm at the bottom).
\item An explicit page applies to every section of a DOCX file.
\item A landscape page turns the whole document, so landscape sections and items in it
stay on the same pages.
